Product change: projects get milestones.

User stories

- As someone who may edit a project, I can create milestones in it, rename them, give or change their target date, close them and reopen them. Anyone who may read the project can list its milestones and see how far each has come.
- A milestone has a name and an optional target date, and is open from its creation until it is closed. Within a project, names are unique without regard to letter case or surrounding spaces — among all of the project's milestones, closed ones included; the same name may be used in another project. A project holds at most `MAX_OPEN_MILESTONES_PER_PROJECT` open milestones (add `export const MAX_OPEN_MILESTONES_PER_PROJECT: number = 10` to `src/config/constants.ts`); creating another, or reopening one, while that many are open is refused. Closed milestones do not count.
- As someone who may edit an issue, I can put it into an open milestone of the issue's own project, move it to another, or take it out — when the issue is created or at any later time. An issue belongs to at most one milestone.
- Progress: a milestone's progress is counted over its live (non-archived) issues, however many there are: the total, the open ones and the closed ones (done or canceled). A milestone is overdue when it is open, its target date lies strictly in the past, and it still has open issues; a milestone without a target date is never overdue.
- Closing: when a milestone is closed, its open live issues are moved to another open milestone of the same project or, when none is named, taken out of any milestone. Its closed and archived issues stay where they are. The assignee of each issue that moved is told once about it; nobody is told about their own closing. Closing a closed milestone, or reopening an open one, is refused.
- Everything about a milestone — creating, changing, closing, reopening, moving issues — is refused for an archived project exactly as editing the project is; listing still works.

Rules

- Guards, in the same spirit as the project writes: an `orgId` outside the actor's workspace → `TenantScopeError`, on every operation. A project or milestone that does not exist in that workspace (including another workspace's) → `NotFoundError` (the one in `src/server/services/_support.ts`). Creating, changing, closing and reopening a milestone take the `project:update` permission on its project; listing and reading take `project:read`; moving an issue takes `issue:update` on that issue — the existing escalation for one's own issue applies — and an archived issue cannot be moved (`AlreadyArchivedError`). An archived project → `AlreadyArchivedError`.
- `MilestoneStateError` (new, `src/lib/milestones.ts`): `export class MilestoneStateError extends Error` with `readonly code = "conflict"` and `readonly reason: "closed" | "already_open" | "too_many_open" | "name_taken"`, thrown for: a closed milestone where an open one is needed (moving an issue into it, naming it as the target of a close, closing it again); reopening an open milestone; the open-milestone cap; a taken name (on create and on rename). `toAppError` maps it to `conflict` and `isDomainError` recognises it.
- Moving an issue into a milestone, at creation or later: the milestone must be one of the issue's project — a milestone of another project or another workspace is `NotFoundError` — and must be open (`MilestoneStateError`). A refusal at creation creates nothing and announces nothing.
- The target of a close must be another open milestone of the same project: a closed one is `MilestoneStateError`; the milestone itself, one of another project or one that does not exist is `NotFoundError`. A refused close changes nothing and announces nothing.
- Listing order: open milestones first, then closed ones; within each group by target date, earliest first, milestones without a target date last; ties by creation, oldest first.
- Events and audit: publish `milestone.created`, `milestone.reopened` (`EventEnvelope & { milestoneId: MilestoneId; projectId: ProjectId }`) and `milestone.closed` (`EventEnvelope & { milestoneId: MilestoneId; projectId: ProjectId; movedToMilestoneId: MilestoneId | null; movedIssueIds: readonly IssueId[] }`), added to `TaskflowEventMap`, exactly once per operation. Record each in the audit log as the actions `milestone.created` / `milestone.closed` / `milestone.reopened`, subject kind `milestone`, subject id = the milestone id, project = its project, actor = who did it — accepted everywhere the existing audit actions and subject kinds are, including the zod schema that validates audit-log filters.
- Alerts: a new notification kind `issue_milestone_changed`, accepted everywhere the existing kinds are, including the zod schema that validates notification-preference updates. When a milestone is closed, each moved issue's assignee receives exactly one alert of that kind for it (through the fan-out, so the recipient's preferences apply as usual); an unassigned issue alerts nobody; the person who closed the milestone is never alerted.
- "Now" is the moment of the call.

Milestones are persisted; a freshly migrated database (the way the test suite builds one) must have whatever you add.

API — add these exports:

In `src/types/common.ts`: `export type MilestoneId = Branded<string, "MilestoneId">` (also a member of `AnyId`).

In `src/types/milestone.ts`:

```ts
export interface Milestone extends Timestamps, TenantScoped {
  readonly id: MilestoneId;
  readonly projectId: ProjectId;
  readonly name: string;
  readonly targetDate: IsoTimestamp | null;
  readonly closedAt: IsoTimestamp | null;
}
export interface MilestoneProgress {
  readonly total: number;
  readonly open: number;
  readonly closed: number;
}
export interface MilestoneWithProgress {
  readonly milestone: Milestone;
  readonly progress: MilestoneProgress;
  readonly overdue: boolean;
}
```

In `src/types/issue.ts`: `Issue` gains `readonly milestoneId: MilestoneId | null`.

In `src/schemas/common.ts`: `export const milestoneIdSchema`, a branded id schema like the others.

In `src/schemas/issue.ts`: `createIssueSchema` gains `milestoneId: milestoneIdSchema.nullable().default(null)`.

In `src/schemas/milestone.ts`:

```ts
export const createMilestoneSchema = z.object({
  orgId: orgIdSchema,
  projectId: projectIdSchema,
  name: z.string().min(1).max(80),
  targetDate: isoTimestampSchema.nullable().default(null),
});
export const updateMilestoneSchema = z.object({
  orgId: orgIdSchema,
  milestoneId: milestoneIdSchema,
  name: z.string().min(1).max(80).optional(),
  targetDate: isoTimestampSchema.nullable().optional(),
});
export const closeMilestoneSchema = z.object({
  orgId: orgIdSchema,
  milestoneId: milestoneIdSchema,
  moveOpenIssuesTo: milestoneIdSchema.nullable().default(null),
});
export const setIssueMilestoneSchema = z.object({
  orgId: orgIdSchema,
  issueId: issueIdSchema,
  milestoneId: milestoneIdSchema.nullable(),
});
```
with the matching `CreateMilestoneInput`, `UpdateMilestoneInput`, `CloseMilestoneInput` and `SetIssueMilestoneInput` types.

In `src/server/services/milestone-service.ts`:

- `export async function createMilestone(actor: Actor, input: CreateMilestoneInput): Promise<Milestone>`
- `export async function updateMilestone(actor: Actor, input: UpdateMilestoneInput): Promise<Milestone>`
- `export async function closeMilestone(actor: Actor, input: CloseMilestoneInput): Promise<{ milestone: Milestone; movedIssueIds: readonly IssueId[] }>` — the ids of the issues that moved, in any order.
- `export async function reopenMilestone(actor: Actor, orgId: OrgId, milestoneId: MilestoneId): Promise<Milestone>`
- `export async function listMilestones(actor: Actor, orgId: OrgId, projectId: ProjectId): Promise<readonly MilestoneWithProgress[]>`
- `export async function getMilestone(actor: Actor, orgId: OrgId, milestoneId: MilestoneId): Promise<MilestoneWithProgress>`

In `src/server/services/issue-service.ts`: `export async function setIssueMilestone(actor: Actor, input: SetIssueMilestoneInput): Promise<Issue>` — resolves to the issue as stored afterwards.

Update any visible test that encodes behaviour this change alters.

